      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight mb-4">
        How to Get Around China: The Complete Transport Guide
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-500">
        High-speed rail, metro, taxi, Didi — how to choose the right transport for every situation in China.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-gray max-w-none">

      <p>China's transport system can feel overwhelming before you arrive — but once you're on the ground, it's actually one of the most efficient and traveler-friendly in the world.</p>

      <p>The key is knowing which option fits each situation. Here's the short version:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Between cities:</strong> High-speed rail (best) or domestic flights (for long distances)</li>
        <li><strong>Within a city:</strong> Metro (fastest) or Didi (most convenient)</li>
        <li><strong>From the airport:</strong> Metro (cheap) or Didi/taxi (door-to-door)</li>
        <li><strong>Short trips:</strong> Didi, taxi, or shared bikes</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>High-Speed Rail (高铁)</h2>
      <p>This is the crown jewel of China's transport system and by far the best way to travel between major cities. A few stats that put it in perspective:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Beijing → Shanghai: <strong>4.5 hours</strong> (vs 2.5 hours by plane, but train stations are in the city center)</li>
        <li>Beijing → Xi'an: <strong>3.5 hours</strong></li>
        <li>Shanghai → Hangzhou: <strong>1 hour</strong></li>
        <li>Guangzhou → Shenzhen: <strong>30 minutes</strong></li>
      </ul>
      <p>The trains are clean, punctual, comfortable, and have decent WiFi on most routes. Second class (二等座) is perfectly fine — think airline premium economy at a fraction of the price.</p>

      <h2>Metro (地铁)</h2>
      <p>Every major Chinese city has an excellent metro system. Beijing and Shanghai have among the longest metro networks in the world. Signs are bilingual (Chinese + English), announcements are in English, and stations have clear maps. It's usually the fastest way to get around within a city, and fares are cheap — typically ¥3-10 ($0.40-1.40).</p>

      <h2>Didi (滴滴)</h2>
      <p>Didi is China's equivalent of Uber, and it's widely used. You can book a ride through the Didi app (which has an English version) or through the mini-programs inside Alipay and WeChat. It works with international credit cards linked to Alipay/WeChat Pay (no Chinese phone number required). This is your best option for door-to-door trips, especially with luggage or in bad weather.</p>

      <h2>Taxis</h2>
      <p>Taxis are everywhere in Chinese cities and relatively cheap. The challenge is communication — not all drivers speak English. Always have your destination written in Chinese characters (and show it to the driver). Use a translation app or a hotel business card for help. Taxis accept cash and mobile payment, but not foreign credit cards.</p>

      <h2>Domestic Flights</h2>
      <p>For very long distances (Beijing → Guangzhou, Shanghai → Chengdu), flying can save time. Major airlines include Air China, China Southern, China Eastern, and Hainan Airlines. Book via Trip.com (English-friendly) or the airline's app. Note that you'll need to arrive at the airport 1.5-2 hours early for domestic flights, and most airports are far from city centers — you'll spend 45-60 minutes just getting there.</p>

      <h2>Shared Bikes</h2>
      <p>For very short trips, shared bikes (HelloBike, Meituan Bike, Didi Bike) are everywhere. Scan the QR code with Alipay or WeChat Pay, ride for a few yuan, and park anywhere. Great for the last kilometer to your destination.</p>

      <h2>Payment for Transport</h2>
      <p>Here's the good news: almost all transport in China can be paid with Alipay or WeChat Pay. Metro systems accept QR code tickets through these apps. Didi bills through the app. Train tickets can be booked online. You generally don't need cash for transport, but keep ¥200-300 just in case.</p>

